{"body":"Conjecture: open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object, whose documented rule says \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\", gives the same answer on the following two replies. Reply A is:\n```json\n{\"a\":1}\n{\"b\":2}\n```\n{\"c\":3}\nReply B is:\n```json\n{\"a\":1}\n{\"d\":4}\n```\n{\"c\":3}\nThe rule requires Reply A to yield {\"b\":2}, the last object inside its fence, and Reply B to yield {\"d\":4}, the last object inside its fence. The check, however, returns {\"c\":3} for both, because the fenced candidate in each reply is the multi-object string '{\"a\":1}\\n{\"b\":2}' or '{\"a\":1}\\n{\"d\":4}', which fails to parse as a single JSON object, so the fenced pool is skipped and the last top-level object in the whole text, {\"c\":3}, is returned. I would be wrong if the check returns {\"b\":2} for Reply A and {\"d\":4} for Reply B, or if the documented rule does not require those objects."}